<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
	xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
	xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
	<!--
	  ============================================================================================
	  REFERENCE / TEMPLATE POM — User-Defined Functions.

	  This is not a buildable module pom; it is the canonical starting point for a NEW gold-standard
	  UDF and the diff baseline for a consistency review of an EXISTING one. Copy this file to
	  <NewUdf>/pom.xml and customize the points marked "CUSTOMIZE" below (base name, description,
	  op-specific deps). Everything else — the release-parameterization structure (STRIIM_HOME /
	  STRIIM_VERSION / STRIIM_SERIES / JAVA_RELEASE, the striim-home-from-env / release-from-env
	  profiles, UDF_VERSION-drives-jar-name-only) is the uniform gold-standard shape and should not
	  vary UDF to UDF without updating this reference (and docs/TESTING-YOUR-JAVA.md) in the same change.

	  UDF_VERSION IS JAR-NAME-ONLY. Unlike an OP (which cache-busts on a package-embedded OP_VERSION —
	  see docs/TESTING-YOUR-JAVA.md), a UDF's package/FQN is FLAT and UNVERSIONED
	  (com.example.<Udf> — the stable public TQL contract) and its CLASS is unversioned
	  (<Udf>.java, no version suffix). UDF_VERSION is required in the pom but used ONLY to name the
	  jar: finalName = ${project.artifactId}${UDF_VERSION}-${STRIIM_SERIES} (e.g. MyUdfV1B-5.4.jar).
	  A version bump changes UDF_VERSION + the changelog only — never the package or the class name.
	  UDFs have no @PropertyTemplate / Striim-Service manifest and no OP-cache-by-package concern (see
	  docs/TESTING-YOUR-JAVA.md).

	  A pure-library UDF pom with the placeholder base name "MyUdf". Replace "MyUdf" throughout with the real UDF's base name.
	  ============================================================================================
	-->
	<modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

	<groupId>com.example</groupId>
	<!-- CUSTOMIZE: the bare base name, e.g. MyUdf. NEVER a version suffix — this is the stable
	     package leaf too (com.example.MyUdf). -->
	<artifactId>MyUdf</artifactId>
	<version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
	<name>MyUdf</name>
	<!-- CUSTOMIZE: one-line description of the function library. -->
	<description>Striim UDF library of &lt;what this library does&gt;.</description>

	<properties>
		<!-- CUSTOMIZE: bump for each shippable jar iteration (V1, V2, ...). USED ONLY IN THE
		     finalName BELOW — it never touches the package, the class name, or the artifactId/name.
		     A version bump changes this property + the changelog, nothing else. -->
		<UDF_VERSION>V1</UDF_VERSION>

		<!-- THE INTEGRATION LAYER IS REQUIRED. A Striim-aware UDF MUST reach the platform
		through a shared seam, not directly. NOTE the gap: UDF builds add-source SampleCommon
		only, so the OpenProcessorCommon seams are not reachable from a UDF today. Adding that
		add-source block is pom work that belongs to the module's upgrade; until then a UDF's
		own injectable interface is the required answer, never a raw platform call.

		PLATFORM COUPLING. These system-scoped jars belong to the platform and are NOT
		shaded into the module: outside a real Striim server they are simply ABSENT, so a
		class loaded from them raises NoClassDefFoundError, an Error that
		`catch (Exception)` does not catch. Reference com.webaction.* from src/main ONLY
		where no shared seam exists.

		Keep platform access behind the shared interfaces listed below so
		the integration harness can substitute them.

		Everything else routes through the OpenProcessorCommon seams below. These are NOT
		add-sourced by this pom: the build-helper block further down add-sources SampleCommon
		only, which is the gap noted above. Until a module adds the OpenProcessorCommon
		add-source, these name the seam to reach for, not one you can call today:
		MetadataAccess           MDCache, MetadataRepository, SecurityInfo,
		runtime.Context, uuid.AuthToken
		TypeResolver             runtime.compiler.*, runtime.TypeGenerator
		BuiltInFuncs             runtime.BuiltInFunc
		PositionPropagation      hand-rolled position attachment
		SecretResolver           decrypting a credential yourself
		AbstractOpenProcessorApp openprocessor.StriimOpenProcessor

		A hand-rolled platform call also cannot be unit tested: a static getInstance()
		cannot be mocked. If no seam exists for what you need, that is a finding. Propose
		one; do not add another hand-roll. A platform lint can check this.
		-->
		<!-- Path to the Striim install whose lib/ supplies system-scoped jars, for Striim-aware UDFs
		     that have them. A pure-library UDF (no system-scoped deps, e.g. this template) doesn't
		     need a Striim install to build — STRIIM_HOME is carried anyway,
		     with the striim-home-from-env profile below, purely so the pom matches the gold-standard
		     shape and no per-developer path can ever leak in. Default MUST stay the neutral
		     /opt/striim — never a personal path. -->
		<STRIIM_HOME>/opt/striim</STRIIM_HOME>

		<!-- Release triad the module builds against by default. Override all three together via
		     STRIIM_VERSION/STRIIM_SERIES/JAVA_RELEASE env vars (release-from-env profile below,
		     derived from your STRIIM_HOME) to target a different Striim
		     release; the defaults
		     here (the 5.4.x baseline) keep this module standalone-buildable with no env set. Only
		     STRIIM_SERIES actually affects THIS module's output (the finalName below) when the UDF is
		     a pure library with no system-scoped deps; STRIIM_VERSION/JAVA_RELEASE are carried for
		     gold-standard shape parity, same rationale as STRIIM_HOME above. A Striim-aware UDF with
		     system-scoped deps uses STRIIM_VERSION for real (in its systemPath jars). -->
		<STRIIM_VERSION>5.4.0.6</STRIIM_VERSION>
		<STRIIM_SERIES>5.4</STRIIM_SERIES>
		<JAVA_RELEASE>17</JAVA_RELEASE>

		<maven.compiler.source>${JAVA_RELEASE}</maven.compiler.source>
		<maven.compiler.target>${JAVA_RELEASE}</maven.compiler.target>

		<!-- CUSTOMIZE: op-specific lib version properties, e.g. for a pure JsonNode library: -->
		<JACKSON_VERSION>2.14.0</JACKSON_VERSION>
	</properties>

	<dependencies>
		<!-- ============================================================================
		     CUSTOMIZE — dependency shape is BY ROLE:

		     PURE-LIBRARY UDF (this template): normal COMPILE-scope deps
		     resolved from Maven Central — portable, no Striim install needed to build. Striim
		     provides the same libs (e.g. Jackson) at runtime, so they are NOT shaded.

		     STRIIM-AWARE UDF: declare Platform/Common/kryo (plus SourceCommons/hazelcast only if
		     actually used) as SYSTEM-scoped deps from ${STRIIM_HOME}/lib — same pattern as
		     OP-reference-pom.xml. Copy ReferenceUdf/pom.xml. kryo is REQUIRED to compile against
		     WAEvent (it implements KryoSerializable), not an optional extra. That pom also adds
		     <additionalClasspathElements> to surefire — see the surefire plugin below — so the unit
		     tests can resolve ${STRIIM_HOME}/lib at test time.

		     EXTERNAL-I/O UDF: as Striim-aware, plus bundle the third-party client libs via a
		     shade/assembly build. If you add a shade step, move the Striim-Build-* manifest entries into
		     the shade plugin's ManifestResourceTransformer — and do NOT add Striim-Module-Name /
		     Striim-Service-* entries, which declare an OP service a UDF does not implement
		     (docs/TESTING-YOUR-JAVA.md).

		     Do not mix the pure-library and Striim-aware patterns in one UDF.
		     ============================================================================ -->
		<dependency>
			<groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
			<artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
			<version>${JACKSON_VERSION}</version>
		</dependency>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
			<artifactId>jackson-core</artifactId>
			<version>${JACKSON_VERSION}</version>
		</dependency>

		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
			<artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
			<version>5.10.2</version>
			<scope>test</scope>
		</dependency>
		<!-- Add mockito-core only if a Striim-aware / external-I/O UDF needs it. -->
	</dependencies>

	<build>
		<!-- Jar name: <artifactId><UDF_VERSION>-<STRIIM_SERIES>.jar, e.g. MyUdfV1-5.4.jar. This is
		     the ONLY place UDF_VERSION is used — the package/class stay unversioned. This is the jar
		     the live harness builds, uploads to UploadedFiles/, and globally LOADs from the test
		     yaml's `udf:` block; an example app.tql never contains a LOAD itself
		     (docs/TESTING-YOUR-JAVA.md). -->
		<finalName>${project.artifactId}${UDF_VERSION}-${STRIIM_SERIES}</finalName>
		<plugins>
			<plugin>
				<groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
				<artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
				<version>3.13.0</version>
				<configuration>
					<source>${maven.compiler.source}</source>
					<target>${maven.compiler.target}</target>
				</configuration>
			</plugin>
			<!--
			  surefire PINNED to 3.2.5: an unpinned surefire silently falls back to 2.12.4, which
			  predates JUnit 5 and reports a vacuous "Tests run: 0" green. Do not remove the pin.

			  CUSTOMIZE (Striim-aware UDFs only): a UDF with system-scoped deps must ALSO put
			  ${STRIIM_HOME}/lib on the TEST classpath, or its unit tests fail to resolve WAEvent
			  et al. ReferenceUdf/pom.xml adds exactly this block; a
			  pure-library UDF (this template's default) does not need it and leaves it out:

			    <configuration>
			        <useManifestOnlyJar>false</useManifestOnlyJar>
			        <additionalClasspathElements>
			            <additionalClasspathElement>${STRIIM_HOME}/lib/*</additionalClasspathElement>
			        </additionalClasspathElements>
			    </configuration>
			-->
			<plugin>
				<groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
				<artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
				<version>3.2.5</version>
			</plugin>
			<!--
			  Stamp the release this jar was built against into MANIFEST.MF so the live-test harness
			  refuses to reuse a jar from a different Striim release / Java level (mirrors the OPs'
			  shade ManifestResourceTransformer entries).
			-->
			<!--
			  WAEvent-aware UDFs ONLY (delete both plugins for a pure-library UDF).
			  add-source compiles SampleCommon's WAEvents into this jar;
			  shade relocates it to a module-unique name, since Striim's global LOAD rejects
			  two jars declaring the same class. outputFile keeps this the only
			  unclassified jar in target/ (the -sources/-javadoc pair is added below).
			-->
			<plugin>
				<groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
				<artifactId>build-helper-maven-plugin</artifactId>
				<version>3.6.0</version>
				<executions>
					<execution>
						<id>add-common-source</id>
						<phase>generate-sources</phase>
						<goals>
							<goal>add-source</goal>
						</goals>
						<configuration>
							<sources>
								<source>${project.basedir}/../../SampleCommon/src/main/java</source>
							</sources>
						</configuration>
					</execution>
				</executions>
			</plugin>
			<plugin>
				<groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
				<artifactId>maven-shade-plugin</artifactId>
				<version>3.5.1</version>
				<configuration>
					<createDependencyReducedPom>false</createDependencyReducedPom>
					<outputFile>${project.build.directory}/${project.artifactId}${UDF_VERSION}-${STRIIM_SERIES}.jar</outputFile>
					<relocations>
						<relocation>
							<pattern>com.example.common</pattern>
							<shadedPattern>com.example.shaded.${project.artifactId}.common</shadedPattern>
						</relocation>
					</relocations>
					<transformers>
						<transformer
							implementation="org.apache.maven.plugins.shade.resource.ManifestResourceTransformer">
							<manifestEntries>
								<!-- Re-stamped: shade rewrites the jar maven-jar-plugin produced. -->
								<Striim-Build-Version>${STRIIM_VERSION}</Striim-Build-Version>
								<Striim-Build-Series>${STRIIM_SERIES}</Striim-Build-Series>
								<Striim-Build-Java>${JAVA_RELEASE}</Striim-Build-Java>
							</manifestEntries>
						</transformer>
					</transformers>
				</configuration>
				<executions>
					<execution>
						<phase>package</phase>
						<goals>
							<goal>shade</goal>
						</goals>
					</execution>
				</executions>
			</plugin>
			<plugin>
				<groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
				<artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
				<version>3.4.1</version>
				<configuration>
					<archive>
						<manifestEntries>
							<Striim-Build-Version>${STRIIM_VERSION}</Striim-Build-Version>
							<Striim-Build-Series>${STRIIM_SERIES}</Striim-Build-Series>
							<Striim-Build-Java>${JAVA_RELEASE}</Striim-Build-Java>
						</manifestEntries>
					</archive>
				</configuration>
			</plugin>

			<!-- ================================================================================
			     API docs and sources, bound to `package`. Output is named from this module's jar
			     base name, landing beside it as <jar>-sources.jar and <jar>-javadoc.jar.

			     Why bind rather than pass the goals on the command line: javadoc is only checked
			     when it is generated, and `mvn package` is the command a developer runs. A broken
			     reference fails that build; so does a @param naming an argument the method does
			     not have. A MISSING @param or @return is only a warning, which is deliberate,
			     because the templates require neither. The check is of form, never of truth: a
			     green build proves the tags RESOLVE, not that they are correct.

			     Do NOT also pass these goals on the command line where this pom is built. The
			     lifecycle already runs them, and a second `default-cli` execution regenerates and
			     re-jars the javadoc for nothing.

			     Both versions are PINNED for determinism, and as a floor against a future Maven 4
			     runner. The pin does not change which version resolves today: 4.0.0-beta-1 declares
			     a Maven 4 prerequisite, so Maven 3.9 rejects it and selects 3.4.0 unaided. Unlike
			     the surefire pin, which prevents a real and current silent-zero-tests green, this
			     one is insurance.

			     These two jars are DEVELOPER artifacts and are never shipped:
			     a packaging step should drop any -sources/-javadoc jar from a customer package.
			     target/ therefore holds more than one jar,
			     so anything counting jars there must exclude them.
			     The live harness is unaffected: it globs target/*-<series>.jar, which neither suffix
			     can match.
			     ================================================================================ -->
			<plugin>
				<groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
				<artifactId>maven-source-plugin</artifactId>
				<version>3.4.0</version>
				<executions>
					<execution>
						<id>attach-sources</id>
						<phase>package</phase>
						<goals>
							<goal>jar-no-fork</goal>
						</goals>
					</execution>
				</executions>
			</plugin>

			<plugin>
				<groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
				<artifactId>maven-javadoc-plugin</artifactId>
				<version>3.12.0</version>
				<executions>
					<execution>
						<id>attach-javadocs</id>
						<phase>package</phase>
						<goals>
							<goal>jar</goal>
						</goals>
					</execution>
				</executions>
			</plugin>

		</plugins>
		<!-- No shade plugin, no Main-Class, no Striim-Module-Name / Striim-Service-* manifest
		     entries — a UDF is a plain library jar of static methods resolved by FQN, not a
		     StriimOpenProcessor service. A Striim-aware UDF that needs a shaded uber-jar (bundled
		     client libs) adds the shade plugin the same way OP-reference-pom.xml does,
		     but still without the Striim-Service manifest entries (those are OP-only). -->
	</build>

	<profiles>
		<!--
		  Override STRIIM_HOME from a STRIIM_HOME environment variable so a hardcoded per-developer
		  path never leaks into the build (a CLI -DSTRIIM_HOME=... beats both). Portability hygiene
		  here for a pure-library UDF; load-bearing for a Striim-aware UDF with system-scoped deps.
		-->
		<profile>
			<id>striim-home-from-env</id>
			<activation><property><name>env.STRIIM_HOME</name></property></activation>
			<properties><STRIIM_HOME>${env.STRIIM_HOME}</STRIIM_HOME></properties>
		</profile>
		<!--
		  Override the release triad from env vars when STRIIM_RELEASE is set — the marker
		  exported alongside STRIIM_VERSION/STRIIM_SERIES/JAVA_RELEASE, so
		  all three resolve together. For a pure-library UDF only STRIIM_SERIES actually changes this
		  module's output (the finalName above); STRIIM_VERSION/JAVA_RELEASE are carried for
		  gold-standard shape parity, same rationale as the STRIIM_HOME profile above.
		-->
		<profile>
			<id>release-from-env</id>
			<activation><property><name>env.STRIIM_RELEASE</name></property></activation>
			<properties>
				<STRIIM_VERSION>${env.STRIIM_VERSION}</STRIIM_VERSION>
				<STRIIM_SERIES>${env.STRIIM_SERIES}</STRIIM_SERIES>
				<JAVA_RELEASE>${env.JAVA_RELEASE}</JAVA_RELEASE>
			</properties>
		</profile>
	</profiles>
</project>
